Z||000000||kornholio||04-18-2001||01:46 PM||||Hi,<BR>Does anyone know if the front caliper bushings for a 86 are the same for any other GM vehicle?  The GM dealer says that they only have one kind.  I brought in my bolt sleeve just to compare and it slid right through it.  There was no snug fit.<P>I went to a parts shop and they said they could order them, but they only came in a kit which was $20.<P>So I went to a brake rebuilding place and they gave me 4 bushings for free.  They said they were only a couple cents anyway.  These bushings were the same size as the GM dealer had.  No use to me.<P>I know that my bolt and sleeve are the right size for the Fiero and they are not worn.  So I need bushings with a smaller hole.  I was just wondering why I get different stories everywhere I check.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Fierowrecker||04-18-2001||06:07 PM||||Hey Korn!<BR>The bushings (rubber rings) are the same...<BR>Did you clean out the groves too well???<BR>Or did you grease them up with plenty of silicone grease?<BR>They should stay in place by themselves, but slide easily...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000002||kornholio||04-18-2001||06:54 PM||||If I slip a brand new bushing onto a brand new sleeve for the Fiero, you can actually see a little gap.  Would I be able to find a sleeve that is a bit bigger on the outside so that it fits the bushing?<P>I thought that Fiero bushings where a different size.<P>So the bushings are all the same?  How come my "un-wornout" sleeve is too small for a snug fit?<P>What's wierd is that the other side is perfectly fine.  I might just take a closer look at the other side to see if it'll give me any clues.<P>Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||04-18-2001||07:28 PM||||Put the bushing in the caliper and check the fit. they get compressed some in there.||||reg||1
